WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.982
This query sequences admissions within each patient.

00:00:02.982 --> 00:00:05.957
LAG retrieves the previous discharge time.

00:00:05.957 --> 00:00:10.697
The outer query subtracts that previous discharge from the current admit time.

00:00:10.697 --> 00:00:13.702
The result is the interval between encounters.

00:00:13.702 --> 00:00:20.527
The first admission for each patient has no previous discharge, so it is excluded in the final stage.

00:00:20.527 --> 00:00:25.892
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:31.907
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:34.242
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:40.142
In a real setting such as an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event,

00:00:40.142 --> 00:00:46.152
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:46.152 --> 00:00:48.297
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.297 --> 00:00:54.240
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
